There are roughly 50 of these errors, all for the same reason: in the header `/usr/include/wx-3.0/wx/string.h`, the implicit conversion of `wxString` to `const char*` is only activated when a certain flag is not set:

``` cpp
    // the first two operators conflict with operators for conversion to
    // std::string and they must be disabled if those conversions are enabled;
    // the next one only makes sense if conversions to char* are also defined
    // and not defining it in STL build also helps us to get more clear error
    // messages for the code which relies on implicit conversion to char* in
    // STL build
#if !wxUSE_STD_STRING_CONV_IN_WXSTRING
    operator const char*() const { return c_str(); }
    operator const wchar_t*() const { return c_str(); }

    // implicit conversion to untyped pointer for compatibility with previous
    // wxWidgets versions: this is the same as conversion to const char * so it
    // may fail!
    operator const void*() const { return c_str(); }
#endif // !wxUSE_STD_STRING_CONV_IN_WXSTRING
```

In the config file `/usr/lib64/wx/include/gtk3-unicode-3.0/wx/setup.h`, one can find the lines

``` cpp
#define wxUSE_STL 1
// ...
#define wxUSE_STD_STRING_CONV_IN_WXSTRING wxUSE_STL
```

I don't really understand the reasoning, because there should be no conflict between the conversion operators, but the documentation of [wxString](http://docs.wxwidgets.org/trunk/classwx_string.html) contains a note

> If you built wxWidgets with `wxUSE_STL` set to 1, the implicit conversions to both narrow and wide C strings are disabled and replaced with implicit conversions to `std::string` and `std::wstring`.

So here is my question: is BOINC not supposed to be built with `wxUSE_STL=1` or could we fix the issue? Basically, one just needs to add `.c_str()` in all the places where errors occur. I think I would be able to do that. However, if you want to rely on implicit conversion instead, I think I'll write a distribution-specific patch.

I spotted a problem where zero-sized (or truncated) output files are returned to the PrimeGrid server.  The final task before calling boinc_finish() of our LLR wrapper code is to boinc_copy() a file into the project directory from the slot directory.

I think there is a hole in the logic of boinc_copy, where if the fread() fails (in my case due to a system interrupt EINTR, but presumably other cases could also occur), it will return 0 but boinc_copy takes this to signify and end-of-file and returns 0.  The file is not (completely) copied and the caller has no way of taking corrective action e.g. back-off and try again, or at least print an error message

I think a better solution than

```
n = fread(buf, 1, sizeof(buf), src);
if (n <= 0) break;
```

is something like:

```
n = fread(buf, 1, sizeof(buf), src);
if (n <= 0) {
   // Either end of file or an error occurred
   if (!feof(src)) retval = ERR_FREAD; // return an error to the caller
   break;
}

This seems to be a fairly rare occurrance, I hit the problem 5 times out of ~1500 tasks, so I'm currently waiting for it to happen again (running with the above fix + some debugging output).  Will update the ticket when that happens.
